---
// src/pages/uis/index.astro  →  arizvi.com/uis
// For the UIS search (Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor, Engineering Technology; founding faculty for the BAS-ET):
// how I teach, what I'd take of the posting's 5 areas, a course I'd build first, how I'd run work-based learning,
// a first year, and one class to try. Not linked from the nav; sends noindex.
// UIS facts are from the posting (Req 1288) and the Board of Trustees item of 19 March 2026. The course is my proposal
// and is labelled as one; the numbers in its labs are invented so they can be checked.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import ClassPlan from '../../components/ClassPlan.astro';
import GaugeDemo from '../../components/GaugeDemo.astro';
import { classes } from '../../data/classes';
import { arrows } from '../../data/bucknell';

const principles = [
  ['Build the course backwards from the outcome.', 'Each course ends in work an employer could use, and every lab builds toward it.'],
  ['Personalize within a defined structure.', 'Everyone runs the same labs. Each student brings the employer problem or the device they care about.'],
  ['Learn on real problems.', 'Real employers and real constraints wherever I can get them. In the BAS-ET that\'s the work-based learning sequence.'],
  ['Test the teaching itself.', 'Mid-term feedback in every course, with changes made before the term ends. A founding cohort can do the same for the whole program.'],
];

// The posting's 5 preferred areas, by what I'd do with each.
const areas = [
  {
    role: 'I\'d lead',
    title: 'Systems integration, project management and applied engineering practice',
    line: 'Requirements -> design to cost -> a schedule with its critical path -> interfaces and an integration test plan. At Amazon I owned Alexa\'s orchestration layer, the one that decides which of ~10,000 skills answers a request, so getting separate systems to work as one is the job I know best.',
    link: { href: '#course', text: 'The course I\'d build first is below' },
  },
  {
    role: 'I\'d lead',
    title: 'Work-based learning coordination and industry partnership development',
    line: 'A 1-page charter the employer signs by week 3, a named mentor, a stand-up every week, and the employer in the room at the end. I\'d start on the employer network before the first students arrive.',
    link: { href: '#wbl', text: 'How I\'d run a placement is below' },
  },
  {
    role: 'I\'d share',
    title: 'Materials, measurement and quality systems',
    line: 'Measurement and quality are mine as far as statistics carries them: whether a gauge can be trusted, control charts, and tests where the pass mark is agreed before the data comes in. The materials labs are a colleague\'s.',
    link: { href: '#try', text: 'Try the gauge class below' },
  },
  {
    role: 'For colleagues',
    title: 'Automation, robotics and industrial controls · Engineering design, CAD/CAM and manufacturing systems',
    line: 'These labs are for the colleagues hired beside me to lead. I\'d teach alongside them, because the project, measurement and employer work runs through their courses too.',
  },
];

// The course I'd offer to build first: 14 weeks, each adding a piece of the final deliverable.
const weeks = [
  { w: 'Weeks 1-2', title: 'What a technical project is', line: 'Scope, requirements, constraints and stakeholders. Students write a 1-page requirements sheet for a small device from a user interview.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 3-4', title: 'Design to cost', line: 'Bills of materials, trade-offs, make or buy. Lab 1: a temperature-screening kiosk\'s bill of materials comes to $90 a unit against a $100 target, and marketing wants a larger display (+$25) and a metal enclosure (+$18). Teams grant what they can, stay at or under $100 and defend 3 trade-offs.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 5-6', title: 'Schedules', line: 'Critical path, buffers and the weekly stand-up. Students mark the critical path on their own schedule and keep a stand-up sheet.' },
  { w: 'Week 7', title: 'Review and midterm', line: 'On paper: a project memo written from a scenario.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 8-9', title: 'Measurement and quality', line: 'Can the gauge be trusted, then control charts. Lab 2 is the class at the bottom of this page.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 10-11', title: 'Systems integration', line: 'Interfaces, test plans and failure modes. Students write an interface list and an integration test plan.' },
  { w: 'Week 12', title: 'Ethics, safety and societal impact', line: 'A 1-page decision record with the trade-off written down.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 13-14', title: 'The employer project', line: 'The charter and the plan, presented to the employer\'s mentor, and a short reflection.' },
];

const plan = [
  { term: 'Before the first students', load: 'The employer network: charters drafted with the first employers, and the transfer map written down with community college faculty.' },
  { term: 'Fall 2027 · the first cohort', load: 'The course above with its labs, and whatever else the cohort needs covered. Every assignment is tagged to the ETAC student outcome it gives evidence for.' },
  { term: 'All year', load: 'A 30-minute stand-up each week with the other founding faculty, and the ABET evidence folder, which I\'d offer to keep for the director.' },
];
---
<Base title="UIS" description="What Ali Rizvi would teach as founding faculty for the Bachelor of Applied Science in Engineering Technology at the University of Illinois Springfield, and how he teaches." noindex>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="intro-grid">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="label">UIS · School of Applied Engineering · Engineering Technology</p>
        <h1>Teaching engineering technology at UIS</h1>
      </div>
      <div class="photo"><img src="/media/headshot.jpg" alt="Ali Rizvi" width="400" height="500" /></div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <Section label="How I teach" id="approach">
    <ol class="principles">
      {principles.map(([head, line]) => <li><strong>{head}</strong> {line}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <p class="muted principles-note">Industrial engineer by training. 14 course sections at Waterloo between 2021 and 2024, ~700 students, classes of 40 to 180. The stories in class come from the work: owning <a href="/work/amazon/">Alexa's orchestration layer</a> at Amazon, <a href="/work/secure-check-n/">SecureCheck-N</a> (a temperature-screening kiosk that shipped during the pandemic), <a href="/work/scramble/">Proteus</a> (52 playing cards with e-ink faces and Bluetooth radios), and the portfolio companies at AMJ Capital. More on the <a href="/teaching/">Teaching</a> page.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section label="What I'd teach" id="areas">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>The posting names 5 areas for the founding cohort. Here they are by what I'd do with each: 2 I'd lead, 1 I'd share, and 2 for the colleagues hired beside me.</p>
    </div></div>
    <ul class="rows bk-wide">
      {areas.map((a) => (
        <li class="grid">
          <div class="label">{a.role}</div>
          <div class="body">
            <span class="title">{a.title}</span>
            <p>{arrows(a.line)}</p>
            {a.link && <p class="sample"><a href={a.link.href}>{a.link.text}</a></p>}
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section label="A course I'd build first · a proposal" title="Engineering project management and systems integration" id="course">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>The curriculum belongs to the director and the cohort. This is the course I'd offer to build first: 14 weeks that end in a project charter and an integration test plan for a real employer problem, with every week adding a piece of it.</p>
    </div></div>
    <ul class="rows compact bk-wide">
      {weeks.map((k) => (
        <li class="grid">
          <div class="label">{k.w}</div>
          <div class="body">
            <span class="title">{k.title}</span>
            <p>{k.line}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">The numbers in the labs are invented so they can be checked. Labs are pass or redo, with feedback inside a week, and the midterm is on paper.</p>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Work-based learning" title="How I'd run a placement" id="wbl">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Every placement starts with a 1-page charter, signed by week 3 by the student, the employer's mentor and me. It says:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>The problem, in the employer's words.</li>
        <li>Who the mentor is, and how often they meet the student.</li>
        <li>What the student gets access to (equipment, data, the site), with the safety and confidentiality terms.</li>
        <li>What the employer gets back, and when.</li>
        <li>How we'll know it worked.</li>
        <li>The dates of the mid-term check-in and the final presentation.</li>
        <li>A plan B if the problem changes.</li>
      </ol>
      <p style="margin-top: 1em;">After that it's a 15-minute stand-up every week, a mid-term check-in with the mentor, and a final presentation with the employer in the room. If an employer goes quiet (it happens, usually around week 9), the charter already names the fallback, and the student switches at that week's stand-up. The grade comes from the charter, the deliverable, the mentor's feedback and a short reflection.</p>
      <p>The posting also asks for community college pathways and the techX dual-credit program at the Capital Area Career Center. For transfer students I'd write the map down course by course with the community college faculty, so a student knows in year 1 of an associate degree what carries over. And I'd want the same employers' mentors in front of techX students too.</p>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

  <Section label="A first year" id="plan">
    <ul class="rows bk-wide">
      {plan.map((p) => (
        <li class="grid">
          <div class="label">{p.term}</div>
          <div class="body"><p>{p.load}</p></div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">A starting point. The curriculum is the director's and the cohort's call, and I'll fit the rest to what the program needs.</p>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Try a class · week 8 of the course above" title="Is the gauge lying?" id="try">
    <ClassPlan plan={classes.gauge} course="uis" pack={false}>
      <p>A shop machines a part that has to come out within 0.25 mm of its nominal size, either way. That's a band 0.50 mm wide, and every part is checked with calipers before it ships.</p>
      <p>In class, 3 students measure 10 parts twice each with the same calipers. A perfect gauge would read the same every time. Theirs doesn't, and how much the readings of 1 part disagree (within a student and between students) is the gauge's standard deviation.</p>
      <p>The number we work out is 6 of those standard deviations as a share of the band. A rough rule of thumb: under 10% is acceptable, 10 to 30% may be acceptable depending on the job, and over 30% is unacceptable. The widget is the debrief. The class's calipers come out around 0.04 mm, and the room has to decide whether parts measured this way can ship.</p>
    </ClassPlan>
    <GaugeDemo />
  </Section>
</Base>
